---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyArea from '@/components/tools/everyday/CalcMoneyArea.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="paint-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyArea client:load variant="paint" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>From room dimensions to tins</h2>
    <p>
      The paint calculator finds wall area as the perimeter multiplied by the ceiling height:
    </p>
    <pre><code>wall area = 2 × (length + width) × wall height
paint     = wall area × coats ÷ coverage per tin</code></pre>
    <p>
      A 14 × 12 ft room with 8 ft ceilings has 416 square feet of wall. Deduct one door and two
      windows (51 sq ft) and you paint 365. Two coats at 350 sq ft per gallon is 2.09 gallons, so
      three tins, because paint is not sold in fractions.
    </p>

    <h2>Coverage: the number that decides the answer</h2>
    <p>
      "350 square feet per US gallon" is the manufacturer's figure for a smooth, sound, previously
      painted wall. Real surfaces are often different.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[440px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Surface</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Sq ft per gallon</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">m² per litre</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Smooth, previously painted</td><td style="text-align:right">350–400</td><td style="text-align:right">10–11</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New plaster or drywall (mist coat)</td><td style="text-align:right">250–300</td><td style="text-align:right">7–8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lightly textured</td><td style="text-align:right">250–300</td><td style="text-align:right">7–8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Heavy texture, brick, render</td><td style="text-align:right">150–250</td><td style="text-align:right">4–6</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ceilings (roller, matt)</td><td style="text-align:right">350–400</td><td style="text-align:right">10–11</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      A US gallon is 3.785 litres. Metric tins are usually 2.5 or 5 litres, so a 5 L tin is about
      1.32 gallons. Set coverage in the unit your tin is sold in.
    </p>

    <h2>How many coats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Same colour on a sound wall: sometimes one coat.</li>
      <li>Normal repaint: two. This is the realistic default, and the calculator starts at it.</li>
      <li>Light over dark, or a strong colour: a tinted primer plus two topcoats. Reds and deep yellows are the worst and can need three.</li>
      <li>Bare plaster: a thinned mist coat first, then two. The mist coat soaks in and does not count as a topcoat.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Doors and windows</h2>
    <p>
      Each door is deducted at 21 square feet (a standard 3 × 7 ft opening) and each window at 15
      (3 × 5 ft). Those are the conventional trade allowances. If your openings are unusually large
      (patio doors, a picture window), set the counts to zero and subtract a wall row, or leave them in
      as extra safety margin.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many decorators deduct nothing for a small room, because the allowance is smaller than the
      wastage. The calculator lets you choose.
    </p>

    <h2>Ceilings, trim and other surfaces</h2>
    <p>
      Set the ceiling field to 1 to add the floor-plan area of each room row to the paintable total:
      length × width, once per coat. Trim is a different product and a different calculation.
      Skirting, architrave and door frames are usually estimated by length, not area, and a litre of
      gloss covers a lot of it. Doors are about 20 square feet per side.
    </p>

    <h2>Buy the whole job at once</h2>
    <p>
      There are two practical reasons. Tinted colours are mixed in batches, and two batches of the same
      colour can look different on a large wall. If you run out three-quarters of the way along a wall,
      the wet edge dries before you get back, and a stripe shows under raking light permanently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Always round up. This calculator does it for you: the difference between 2.09 gallons and three
      tins is a few units of currency, and the difference between finishing and not finishing is an
      afternoon.
    </p>

    <h2>A rough order of work</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Measure each room: length, width and ceiling height.</li>
      <li>Set coats and coverage realistically for your surface.</li>
      <li>Count doors and windows.</li>
      <li>Read the tin count, add the ceiling if you are doing it.</li>
      <li>Add primer separately if you are covering bare plaster or a dark colour.</li>
      <li>Export the list and take it to the shop.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Works in an empty house with no signal</h2>
    <p>
      After the page loads, it runs fully on your device, which helps in an unfurnished room with a tape
      measure and one bar of signal. Saving is off by default, and when you turn it on it writes only to
      this browser.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
